# Vision Code Lexicon

This document defines a strict, non-overlapping meaning for selected terms used across `vision/`.
Only words with an unambiguous single definition are included here.

## How to judge a word (mandatory)

When asked whether a word fits this lexicon or “has a definition,” judge **meaning in `vision/` code**, not whether the token appears in lexicon.

**Lexicon headwords name one concept as a single word** (or an established pattern token such as `` `_from_` `` or `` `a_*` ``). Named paradigms that are one concept in prose (`moving_bar`, `spot`) may appear as compounds. Glue-compound headwords that join two independent words without forming one concept are forbidden. Exception: ``val_from`` (see ``### val_from``). Forbidden: hyphen spellings inside lexicon text (`val-from`).

**Banned words never get a headword.** A token that is only a forbidden substitute (listed under another concept’s **Forbidden**) must **not** have its own ``### \`word\`` entry — not even an entry whose body says “Forbidden / do not use.” Ban it only by naming it under the canonical concept’s Forbidden line. Creating ``### \`name\`` / ``### \`gain\`` / ``### \`polarity\`` (etc.) solely to forbid them is itself a lexicon violation.

**Evidence:** List every function, method, class, attribute, constant, CLI/opts key, and variable/parameter whose name contains the word (compounds included: `foo_word`, `word_bar`, `WordBaz`). If meanings differ, partition the list into one class per distinct definition; put unrelated homographs (library APIs, other biophysics, …) in an “other” class — still list them.

Then answer only:

1. **Standalone definition?** Can this concept (per class under review) be written as a `### \`word\`` entry (Definition / Example / Forbidden)? Yes = one coherent meaning; no = generic pattern / fragment / prose glue / banned substitute. Multiple classes → say how many standalones.
2. **Collision?** Does any class duplicate another lexicon concept (or another in-code concept that needs its own entry)? Same role under two names → collision (name both).

**Answer shape:** (1) name inventory by meaning class; (2) standalone yes/no + one-line meaning per class; (3) collision yes/no + other term(s). Do not answer with only “it appears / doesn’t appear in the lexicon.”

## Nouns (A-Z)

### `a` / `a_*`

Definition: Multiplicative network / schema parameter — a tunable (or fixed) factor applied inside the model, always named with prefix ``a_``. Covers forward gains (`a_in`, `a_out`, `a_h`, `a_ca`, `a_sti_radius`, `a_syn_exc`, `a_syn_inh`) and cost affine `a_gt`. Bounds bag `A_LO` / `A_HI`.
Example: `params["a_out"]`, `v_out = relu(v − v_th) · a_out`, `ScatterConn(..., a_syn_exc=A_SYN_EXC, a_syn_inh=A_SYN_INH)`, `gt_aff = a_gt * gt + bias_gt`
Forbidden: `gain` (including `GAIN_*`, prose “gain param”); do not name these with `scale` or `weight`. Do not use bare `a` as a local for other quantities.

### `all`

Definition: Universal-quantifier / leftover token — not a collection prefix. Allowed only as: (1) train-mode leftover key ``'all'`` (nodes not claimed by an explicit mode); (2) CLI universal token such as ``all_param`` (one mode text applied to every param name); (3) boolean predicates whose head is not a collection noun (``all_active``, ``all_columned``).
Example: `param_modes["bias_gt"] = parse_param_mode_text("fixed=all")`, `all_param`, `all_mode`, `all_init`, `all_idx`
Forbidden: as a prefix on collection / bag names — never `all_hexes`, `all_nodes`, `all_params`, `all_gt`, `all_v`, `all_traces`, …. Use the plural noun alone (`hexes`, `nodes`, `gts`, `vals`, `traces`), or `run_params` / `run_adams` for per-run bags. Do not write `all_` + plural (`all_stis`) or `all_` + singular collection (`all_sti`).

### `batch` / `batches`

Definition: A batch dimension used to represent parallel sti samples (e.g., which sti instance a cost entry belongs to).
Example: `active_batches = pack.entry_batches.unique(sorted=True)`, `batch_idx`, `batch_sums`
Forbidden: single-letter batch shorthands (`b`, `_b`, `n_b`) — use `batch_idx` for a batch index, `batch_<noun>` for per-batch collections (e.g. `batch_sums`, `batch_n_nodes`), `n_batch` for how many batches, `sti_batch_idx` when indexing `i_sti` batch slots separately from readout `batch_idx`.

### `cell` / `cells`

Definition: A neuron type label from the connectome (e.g. `"L1"`, `"T4a"`), grouping multiple nodes that share the same biological identity.
Example: `connectome.cells` (connectome vocabulary mapping node indices to cell types)
Forbidden: do not use `node` to refer to a cell type. `cell` is a connectome type label; `node` is a specific numbered instance in the network. Do not use `name` / `names` / `cell_name` / `*_by_name` / `idx_from_name` for this — use `cell` / `cells` / `idx_from_cell` / `*_by_cell`.

### `center` / `centers`

Definition: Spot / RF / cost readout locus at Euclidean radius 0 — the sti anchor hex (`du=dv=0`), the `entry_radii==0` slot, the RF axis index 0, or the plot row / time trace for that radius. Compounds: `spot_centers` (simultaneous sti anchor hexes), `center_entry_mask`, `center_only`, `center_hex` (sole cost hex when `cost_radius==0`), `pack_center_mask`, `build_spot_center_readout`, `RF_CENTER_RADIUS`, `gt_center`, `v_readout_center`.
Example: `center_entry_mask = (du == 0) & (dv == 0)`, `spot.centers`, `center_only=True`, `RF_CENTER_RADIUS = 0`
Forbidden: do not use `center` for bar-trail midpoint, histogram bin midpoint, node `(u,v)` lookup, or hex-patch vertex bounds — use `mid` or non-`center` hex coords. Do not use `r0` / `R0` as naming tokens (see coding-rules). Library matplotlib layout kwargs (`xanchor='center'`, `ha='center'`) may remain.

### `col` / `cols`

Definition: A horizontal subplot-grid index in figure code only — which panel slot within one plot `row` (matplotlib `GridSpec` column, trace column within a component figure).
Example: `n_col`, `col_idx`, `N_COL_GT`, `fig.add_subplot(gs[row_idx, col])`, `spec.n_col`
Forbidden: connectome `column` / `column_id`; CSV table field; hex position; cost `entry` mask — do not use `col` outside plot code. Library matplotlib kwargs like `sharex="col"` may remain.

### `column` / `columns`

Definition: A connectome optic-lobe position identifier — the biological column a neuron is assigned to (`column_id` on disk / per record; in-memory bag `connectome.column_ids`), mapped to hex `(u, v)` via column-assignment tables.
Example: `connectome.column_ids`, `majority_column_id`, `columns.csv.gz`, `partner_column_id`, `u_by_column_id`, JSON/CSV field `"column_id"`
Forbidden: do not use `column` for a CSV output-table field — use `field`. Do not use `column` for a hex `(u, v)` position — use `hex`. Do not use `col` as a naming token for connectome columns — spell `column` / `column_id` (disk) / `column_ids` (in-memory bag). Do not use `field` for sti view geometry or ``--param`` keys — use `view` / `key`.

### `connectome` / `connectomes`

Definition: The loaded biological neural-circuit dataset (FAFB), instantiated as a `Network` object; the source of `cell`, `syn`, `hex` vocabulary and connectivity structure.
Example: `connectome = backend.network` (the connectome object used throughout train)
Forbidden: do not use `network` to refer to the connectome object or its biological contents (`cell`, `syn`, `hex`). `network` names the simulation graph, not the biological dataset. Do not abbreviate the connectome object as `C`.

### `contrast` / `contrasts`

Definition: Bright vs dark sti variant (``"bright"`` | ``"dark"``) for spot / moving-bar tasks — which light polarity the sti uses, not a ±1 multiplier.
- ``contrast`` (singular): one token ``"bright"`` or ``"dark"`` (param, pack info field, MovingBarSpec field, dict key).
- ``contrasts`` (plural): a list/set/tuple of those tokens.
The ±1 for a contrast is ``contrast_sign`` (see ``sign``), not ``contrast`` itself.
Example: `contrast_sign(contrast)`, `SPOT_CONTRASTS`, `MOVING_BAR_CONTRASTS`, `GRUNTMAN_CONTRASTS`, `MovingBarSpec.contrast`, `contrast_for_task`, `contrasts=(contrast,)`, pack info ``"contrast"``, figure keys `{contrast: readout}`
Forbidden: `polarity` (including `polarity_sign` and prose “polarity”). Do not use `contrast` for a ±1 — that is `sign` / `contrast_sign`. Do not use `contrast` for intensity / adapt gates or other non-bright/dark senses.

### `cost` / `costs`

Definition: The scalar optimization objective value computed from readouts versus ground truth (loss).
Example: `best_cost = float(final_costs[int(np.argmin(final_costs))])`

### `data`

Definition: The set of parameter output files (`.npy`, `.npz`, `train_opts.json`, etc.) saved under the `data/` subfolder of a run directory.
Example: `os.makedirs(run_data_dir(outdir), exist_ok=True)`
Forbidden: `artifact`, `output` — use `data` instead. Do not use `data` for in-memory forward or plot worksets — use `readout`.

### `default`

Definition: Numeric/config literal source — scope split by tree.
- **`vision/simulation/**`** (numbered core, figure, analyze, run, experiment): allowed only as the module/file name ``default_params`` — the sole numeric literal bag for train, figure, analyze, and run layers. Simulation code defines no ``default_*`` / ``DEFAULT_*`` constants; literals live in ``default_params`` and are imported by role name (``BAR_RADIUS``, ``SYN_SIGN_BINS``, …) or via ``resolve_*`` at CLI/session assembly.
- **`vision/connectome/**`**: connectome-build literals may use noun ``DEFAULT_*`` constants (``DEFAULT_NETWORK_RUN``, ``DEFAULT_RADIUS``, ``DEFAULT_SIDE``, …) for CLI defaults and reference geometry. Not the simulation ``default_params`` module.
Example: `from default_params import RUN_PATH`, `default_params.NEURON_SCHEMA['optimizable']`; connectome `path.DEFAULT_NETWORK_RUN`, `build_hex.DEFAULT_RADIUS`; figure CLI `default=DEFAULT_NETWORK_RUN` importing from connectome ``path``.
Forbidden: In **vision/simulation/** only — ``default`` / ``defaults`` and any project identifier containing ``default`` (``default_*``, ``*_default``, ``DEFAULT_*``, ``_default_*``) except the module name ``default_params``. Do not coin verb/compound substitutes for connectome ``DEFAULT_*`` in simulation (``FALLBACK_*``, ``BUILD_*``, ``BASE_*``, ``ASSIGN_*``, …). Library/API spellings may remain unchanged (``argparse`` keyword ``default=``, dataclass ``default_factory``, ``dict.setdefault``, help ``%(default)s``).

### `edge` / `edges`

Definition: A directed connection between two nodes in the network, carrying a signed `weight` (`edge_weights`) derived from connectome synapse counts (`syn_sign * n_syn` or `syn_sign`).
Example: `source_indices = np.empty(len(edges), dtype=np.int64)`, `edge_weights`
Forbidden: do not use `syn` to refer to a network edge or its idx. `edge` is a network connection instance; `syn` is a connectome biological synapse.

### `entry` / `entries`

Definition: One cost comparison unit in a `Pack`: a (cell × radius) or (cell × PD/ND) record. Pack aligns bags `entry_nodes`, `gts`, `cost_scales`, `entry_batches`, `entry_radii` (one slot per entry; `entry_radii` is spot Euclidean). The cost MSE is a scaled sum over entries with ``entry_mask`` True.
Example: `entries = _active_entry_indices(work, session, batch_idx=batch_idx)`
Forbidden: do not use `node` to refer to a cost entry. `entry` is a cost-layer record; `node` is a network neuron idx.

### `f` / `fs`

Definition: Frequency in hertz (Hz) — oscillation / spectral peak / band limits (e.g. trace FFT analysis). Compound as `<role>_f` (`peak_f`, `min_osc_f`); collection / FFT axis `fs`.
Example: `peak_f`, `min_osc_f`, `TRACE_OSC_MIN_F`, `mask = (fs >= min_osc_f) & (fs <= max_osc_f)`, CLI `--min-f`
Forbidden: `freq`, `freqs`, `freq_hz`, `frequency`, `hz` as naming tokens for this concept.

### `fit` / `fits`

Definition: A cell type that participates in the train cost via ground-truth targets (the model is optimized against these cells). Compounds: `fit_cell`, `mirror_fit` (source cell whose gt is mirrored onto other types), `fit_gts`.
Example: `mirror_fit='L1'`, `fit_gts(...)`, “fit cells” for the GT/cost cell set
Forbidden: do not use `fit` as a verb for the Adam update loop — use `optimize`. Do not use `fit` for the overall process / package — use `train`. Do not name optimize results with `fit` (`z_fit`, `fit_parts`, “fitted parameters”) — use `z_best`, `final_parts`, `parameters`.

### `degree` / `degrees`

Definition: An angular or visual-field quantity measured in degrees (°) — used for bar width, lane pitch, hex vertex positions, and motion-axis view bounds.
Example: `x_deg, y_deg = build_hex.xy_deg_from_uv(u, v)`
Forbidden: do not use `extent` to refer to a degree quantity. `degree` is angular; `radius` is a hex ring count.

### `gt` / `gts`

Definition: Ground truth target signal(s) used as the train/evaluation reference (e.g. spot/moving-bar response waveforms).
- ``gt`` (singular): one target waveform — one entry’s `(T,)` trace, one cell’s one-radius curve, a spot cell’s `(RF_N_RADII, n_t)` array, or a plot series that draws a single GT line.
- ``gts`` (plural): a stack/bag of those waveforms — Pack/SpotGt/MovingBarGt field ``gts`` shaped `(n_cost, T')`, or any `(n, T)` / `(n_cells, …)` tensor that holds multiple gt rows.
Example: `mirror_gt = pack.gts[entry_i:entry_i+1]`, `gts = torch.cat([pack.gts, extra_gts], dim=0)`, `pack.gts`, `_gather_cost_time(..., gts)`, `load_gt(...)`, `fit_gts(...)`
Forbidden: naming a multi-row gt tensor ``gt`` (use ``gts``). Naming a single waveform ``gts``. Do not use ``all_gt``. Do not suffix spot GT with ``_rt`` (`gt_rt`, `fit_gt_rts`, `spot_gt_rts`) — radius×time shape is implied; use ``gt`` / ``gts``. Do not name pre-GT components ``rf_gt`` / ``ir_gt``. Module paths (`task.spot.gt`), CLI ``--gt``, and params ``a_gt`` / ``bias_gt`` are compounds — not this singular/plural rule.

### `group` / `groups`

Definition: A biological grouping in cell or DSI semantics — e.g. a cell-type family band in plot layout (`cell_plot_rows`), or a PD/ND entry list pair in one DSI comparison unit. Not a cost-layer accumulate term.
Example: `pos_groups`, `neg_groups` in `assemble_moving_bar_dsi_groups`; `cell_plot_rows`, `CELL_PLOT_ROWS`
Forbidden: do not use `group` for cost entries accumulated into sub-costs — use `part`. Do not use `group` for sti batch tuples forwarded together — use `batch_set`.

### `hex` / `hexes`

Definition: A single hexagonal spatial position in the connectome, identified by its axial coordinates `(u, v)`.
Example: `for hex in moving_bar_cost_hexes(connectome, cost_radius=cost_radius): ...`
Forbidden: do not use `column` to refer to a hex position. `hex` is a spatial `(u, v)` location; `column` is a connectome position id (`column_id` / bag `column_ids`).

### `id` / `ids`

Definition: An external identifier from persisted or connectome source data (e.g. FAFB node `id` in `network.json`, `column_id`, `root_id`) — not an in-memory array position.
Example: `node_ids`, `node_from_id`, `column_id` (disk/JSON), `connectome.column_ids` (in-memory bag)
Forbidden: do not use `idx` to refer to an external/source identifier. `id` is a key stored in connectome or sidecar data; `idx` is a position in an in-memory ordered axis.

### `idx` / `indices`

Definition: A position in an ordered in-memory axis used for `[...]` indexing (simulation node 0..N-1, batch slot, time step, cost entry slot, part slot, etc.).
Example: `node_idx`, `source_indices`, `entry_batches`, `part_indices`
Forbidden: do not use `id` to refer to an in-memory array position — external node keys must be mapped through `node_from_id` first. Do not use `index` or `ix` as naming tokens; they denote the same role as `idx` / `indices`. Do not use bare `i` as an idx abbrev in compounds (`i_from_name` → `idx_from_cell`). Do not use `row` for a cost-entry mask or CSV record — use `entry` / `entry_mask` or `field`. Do not use `t_idx`, `step_idx`, or `time_idx` for a time sample index — use bare `t` (see `t` / `n_t`).

### `implementation`

Definition: Integration layer that wires the pieces of one large action (session, cost, optimization, persistence, …) into a path that runs through successfully before the top-level ``run`` driver. Module ``train.implementation`` (e.g. ``run_train``).
Example: `fname, outdir, session, result = run_train(...)`
Forbidden: `implement` — use `implementation`. Do not use for the top-level plot/CLI driver (`simulation/run.py`) or for argparse-only parsing (`train.cli`).

### `in`

Definition: Total input to a neuron — light sti (`sti`) plus presynaptic/synaptic contributions. At current level (borst): `i_sti + Σ gE`; at voltage level (hp_lp): `v_in = v_syn + v_sti`. Synaptic input `a_*` param: `a_in`.
Example: `v_in = v_syn + v_sti`, `params["a_in"]`, `C dv/dt = i_sti + Σ g_i (E_i − v)`
Forbidden: `input`, `is_input`, `_in` on sti-only quantities (`u_in`, `v_in` for sti hex coords, `pr_idx`, `n_input_nodes`). Do not use `in` for light sti alone — use `sti`.

### `iter` / `iters`

Definition: One optimizer iteration (a single Adam gradient update), used to count train progress (`n_iter`, `global_iter`, `checkpoint_interval`).
Example: `n_iter = N_ITER_GPU if cuda_available else N_ITER_CPU`

### `map` / `maps`

Definition: A lookup table from one key space to another — a dict or idx-array pair (e.g. node idx → cell, hex idx → node idx, cell → integer idx). Pattern `value_from_key` (never `key_to_value`).
Example: `cell_from_node(nodes_by_cell)` → `dict[int, str]`; `node_from_id`, `idx_from_cell`, `idx_from_radius`
Forbidden: `_to_` in map function / attribute / local names (`node_to_cell`, `id_to_node`, `name_to_i`). Do not use `name` / `names` in map identifiers (`idx_from_name`, `*_by_name`) — use the real source noun (`cell`, `segment`, `radius`, …).

### `mask` / `masks`

Definition: A boolean or 0/1 selector along an axis marking which positions participate (cost `entry`, time sample, sti radius slot, node, hex, …). Compound as `<axis>_mask` (`entry_mask`, `cost_time_mask`, `a_sti_radius_mask`, `hex_mask`).
Example: `a_sti_radius_mask`, `build_a_sti_radius_mask(...)`, `pack.cost_time_mask`, `entry_mask = …`, `mask = (fs >= min_osc_f) & (fs <= max_osc_f)`
Forbidden: `gate` — do not use `gate` for this participation selector (`a_sti_radius_gate`, “gated slots”). Biophysical channel gating kinetics (e.g. i_h open-state ODEs `_gate_ss`, `_i_h_gate_step`) are a different concept and may keep `gate`. Do not use `active` or `present` for a dense per-element boolean array — use `mask`. Do not combine `active` and `mask` in one identifier.

### `mid` / `mids`

Definition: Geometric or temporal midpoint along a path, lane, or bin axis — not Euclidean radius 0. Moving-bar transit: trail midpoint in degrees (`trail_mid`), time sample between entry and exit (`mid_t`, return slot ``mid`` of ``moving_bar_transit_times``, snapshot labels ``"mid"``). Histogram: bin midpoint (`bin_midpoints`).
Example: `trail_mid = origin + 0.5 * (...)`, `start_t, mid_t, exit_t = moving_bar_transit_times(...)`, `labels = ("start", "mid", "exit")`, `bin_midpoints = 0.5 * (edges_bins[:-1] + edges_bins[1:])`
Forbidden: do not use `mid` for sti-on hex / Euclidean radius 0 — use `center`. Do not use `center` for these midpoints.

### `mode` / `modes`

Definition: A string token selected from a fixed enumeration, identifying which variant of a configurable behaviour is active (e.g. `syn_mode ∈ {"per_cell","per_edge"}`, segment ``mode`` ∈ {"indi","shared","fixed","frozen"}``, schema ``z_mode`` ∈ {"linear","log","inv"}). Plural ``modes`` is the bag mapping each mode token to its node/slot index list (or a collection of such tokens).
Example: `mode = normalize_syn_mode(syn_mode)`, `for mode in PARAM_MODES: s[mode] = list(modes[mode])`, `segment.get("z_mode", "linear")`
Forbidden: `bucket`, `mode_bucket` — never name a mode token or the modes bag with ``bucket``. Do not use ``z_map`` for the z↔param encoding — that is ``z_mode``.

### `moving_bar` / `moving_bars`

Definition: The moving-bar sti paradigm (and its related task variants) used to generate inputs and corresponding targets.
Example: `if pack.task in MOVING_BAR_TASKS: ...`

### `n` / `n_<noun>`

Definition: Integer cardinality — how many of an object exist or contribute at one scope. Use the compound `n_<noun>` when the counted object is not obvious (`n_nodes`, `n_edges`, `n_keys`, `n_syn`, `n_partner`, `n_self`, `n_hexes`). Bare `n` is allowed only as a short-lived local when the noun is already fixed (`mean = sum_ / n`).
Example: `n_nodes = len(nodes)`, `ca_n[(cell, spec)]`, `n_syn` on an edge
Forbidden: `count`, `counts` — never as naming tokens for cardinality. Do not use `total` for a cardinality — `total` is cost-only. Do not use `sum` / `sums` for a cardinality — those are accumulated values.

### `network` / `networks`

Definition: The simulation graph used during train — the set of nodes and directed edges instantiated from the connectome into `ScatterConn` tensors for forward computation.
Example: `net = backend.network`
Forbidden: do not use `connectome` to refer to the simulation graph or its tensor representation (`node`, `edge`). `connectome` names the biological source dataset, not the in-memory train structure. Do not coin project names with `scatter` (e.g. `_scatter`, `_scatter_cost_parts`); keep class `ScatterConn` and library APIs (`tensor.scatter_add_`, `ax.scatter`) only.

### `node` / `nodes`

Definition: A specific neuron instance in the network (indexed 0..N-1), used in tensors shaped `(N,)` or `(B,T,N)`.
Example: `pack.entry_nodes.shape[0]`
Forbidden: do not use `cell` to refer to a node idx or node tensor. `node` is a numbered network instance; `cell` is a connectome type label.

### `opt` / `opts`

Definition: A set of input options (often parsed from CLI or sidecar JSON) used to configure sessions, stis, or costs.
Example: `opt = session.train_opts or {}`

### `optimization`

Definition: The Adam / staged-lr update loop that minimizes `cost` (module ``train.optimization``: `optimize_staged`, `do_many_runs`). One middle step of a `train`; not the whole process and not the cost scalar itself.
Example: `z_best, opt_state = optimize_staged(...)`, `result = do_many_runs(session, ...)`
Forbidden: do not use `opt` for this concept (`opt` / `opts` are input options only). Do not use `train` as a verb for this loop — use `optimize`. Do not name the returned ``z`` with `fit` — use `z_best`.

### `out`

Definition: Presynaptic output from a neuron — thresholded membrane signal multiplied by `a_out`: `v_out = relu(v − v_th) · a_out`. Connectome node JSON key `"output"` marks readout/output nodes when set.
Example: `v_out`, `params["a_out"]`, `"output": False` in `network.json` nodes
Forbidden: `output` for persisted run files or sidecar tables — use `data`. Do not use `out` for total membrane input — use `in`. Do not use `out` for light sti — use `sti`.

### `pack` / `packs`

Definition: One task's train cost-spec container — that task's `i_sti`, cost `entry` indices, `gt` traces, cost `scale`s, and related timing/DSI fields — used by `forward` / `cost` / `plot`. Type name `Pack`; locals/params `pack`; `session.packs` holds one per task; `session.primary_pack` is the first task's pack.
Example: `pack = session.primary_pack`, `session.pack_for("spot_bright")`, `pack.task`, `pack.gts`, `pack_t_onset(pack)`, `override_pack`
Forbidden: `readout`, `ReadoutPack`, `primary_readout`, `session.readouts` — those names must not refer to this object. Do not use `pack` for the in-memory forward→plot assembly (that is `readout`). Do not use `pack` for the verb of stuffing parameters into `z` (schema packing). Do not store the task id on Pack as ``name`` — field is ``task``.

### `param` / `params`

Definition: A single named scalar/vector quantity in the model (the value used by dynamics after ``param_from_z``). Optimizable bag optional string ``param`` holds space-separated ``KEY[.NODES]=VALUE`` tokens for that segment. Per-node init bag on a segment is ``inits`` (plural; scalar default remains ``init``).
Example: `p["a_gt"]`, `segment["inits"]`, optimizable ``param="val.L1=0.5"``, `param_from_z`, `z_from_param`
Forbidden: do not use ``physical`` for this z↔param value — use ``param``.

### `part` / `parts`

Definition: One named sub-cost of the total train cost, identified by a `part_key` string (e.g. `"spot_bright_R8_r0"`, `"moving_bar_bright_PD"`) and holding its own scalar MSE tensor. Multiple cost `entry` records sharing the same `part_key` are accumulated via `part_indices` into one `part`. The total cost is `Σ W·part / Σ W` over parts with non-zero scale.
Example: `parts[part_key] = part` in `calc_cost_parts`

### `prep`

Definition: Plot-pipeline wall-clock phase before draw — forwarding / assembling readouts that feed a figure (timed as ``prep_s`` / ``PlotTimer`` prep; log field ``prep=…s``). Noun for that timing slot only — not a verb for model hooks.
Example: `readout_prep_s(...)`, `TraceReadout.prep_s`, `PlotTimer(prior_prep=...)`, `timer.end_prep()`, `log_plot_elapsed(..., prep=...)`
Forbidden: `prepare` as a naming token for this timing phase. Do not use `prep` for model ``normalize_i_sti`` / sti shaping, ``pre_steady``, or pre-onset time windows (`ms_pre`, `pre_end`, `show_pre`). Do not use `draw` / `save` for this phase.

### `radius` / `radii`

Definition: An integer hex ring count labelling a position or area size — used for connectome geometry (`connectome.meta["radius"]`), spot footprint (`spot_radius`), sub-spot shift neighbourhood (`shift_radius`), bar width+spacing (`bar_radius`), and cost hex-disc (`cost_radius`). Does not refer to Euclidean or angular distances.
Example: `connectome_radius = int(connectome.meta.get("radius", -1))` (JSON key `"radius"` in `network.json`)
Forbidden: do not use `extent` to refer to any hex ring count. `radius` is the sole term for hex ring counts.

### `readout` / `readouts`

Definition: An in-memory assembled forward result for one task scope — cost `trace`s plus entry indices, timing, and plot metadata between `forward` and `plot`. Distinct from persisted run `data` and from the cost-spec `pack`.
Example: `_forward_spot_readout`, `readout["plot_traces"]`, `_spot_v_readout_from_readout`, `build_spot_center_readout`, `SpotTraceReadout`
Forbidden: `data` — use `data` only for files under a run's `data/` folder. Do not name the container `rows`, `forward_data`, or `bundle_data`. Do not use `readout` for the connectome biological column — use `column` / `column_ids`. Do not use `readout` / `ReadoutPack` / `primary_readout` / `session.readouts` for the cost-spec container — that is `pack` / `Pack` / `primary_pack` / `session.packs`.

### `ir` / `irs`

Definition: Spot impulse-response time component — pre-GT temporal kernel ``ir(t)`` per cell, shape ``(n_t,)`` or stacked ``(n_cell, n_t)``. Assembled spot GT is ``gt[r,t] = rf(r) × ir(t)``; ``ir`` is not GT. Source builders use ``build_ir_<source>`` / ``load_ir_<source>``: ``build_ir_lti`` (sti → bandpass/LP), ``load_ir_arenz`` (Arenz CSV).
Example: `load_rf_ir(...) → (rf, rf_peak_scale, ir)`, `build_ir_lti(cell, u, delta_ms=...)`, `load_ir_arenz(...)`, `normalize_ir(...)`, `IR_delay_ms[cell]`, `t_delay_from_ir(...)`, `_gt_from_rf_ir(rf, ir)`
Forbidden: ``ImpR``, ``impr``, ``im_arenz``, ``ir_gt``, ``ImpR_gt``, ``IR_SHIFT``, ``t_lag`` — ``ir`` is a generation component, not a gt alias. Do not use ``ir`` for total membrane input — use ``in``. Use ``IR_delay_ms`` (ms dict keyed by cell) and sample ``t_delay``, not sample-count ``IR_SHIFT`` / ``t_lag``.

### `rf` / `rfs`

Definition: Spot receptive-field spatial component — pre-GT radial kernel ``rf(r)`` per cell, shape ``(RF_N_RADII,)`` or stacked ``(n_cell, RF_N_RADII)``. Assembled spot GT is ``gt[r,t] = rf(r) × ir(t)``; ``rf`` is not GT. Literal bag ``RF_SCALE`` (unsigned peak-normalized samples keyed by cell); builder ``build_rf``.
Example: `load_rf_ir(...) → (rf, ir)`, `build_rf(cell)`, `RF_SCALE[cell]`, `RF_SIGN[cell]` (membership only), `_spot_readout_a_radius(rf, radius)`
Forbidden: ``RecF``, ``recf``, ``rf_gt``, ``RecF_gt``, ``RF_GRUNTMAN_ROW``, ``build_rf_row``, ``rf_row``, ``rf_peak_scale``, ``gain`` — ``rf`` is a generation component, not a gt alias. Do not multiply ``RF_SIGN`` into ``RF_SCALE`` / ``build_rf``. Do not special-case cell names (e.g. ``startswith("T4")``) in rf/ir loaders.

### `row` / `rows`

Definition: A vertical subplot-grid index in figure code only — one plot panel band (component figure row, cell layout band, RF+time stack band).
Example: `n_row`, `row_idx`, `CELL_PLOT_ROWS`, `plot_row_idxs`, `row_shared_ylim`, `fig.add_gridspec(n_row, n_col)`
Forbidden: CSV record row; cost `entry` / `entry_mask`; `i_sti` batch slot; GT lookup-table row index; in-memory forward container — do not use `row` outside plot code. Library pandas / CSV `row` locals in one-off parsers may remain if the noun is not exported.

### `run` / `runs`

Definition: One independent train execution from initialization to convergence, producing one set of parameters.
Example: `for i in range(n_run): ...`

### `schema` / `schemas`

Definition: The parameter schema (segment list) describing how params are packed into the trainable space `z` and decoded back. Segment key ``z_mode`` ∈ ``{linear, log, inv}`` selects the z↔param encoding (not a numeric multiplier).
Example: `for segment, start, stop in schema_segments(schema): ...`, `segment.get("z_mode", "linear")`, `param_from_z`, `z_from_param`
Forbidden: do not use ``scale`` or ``z_map`` for the z↔param encoding key — that is ``z_mode``. Do not use ``physical`` for the decoded param value.

### `segment` / `segments`

Definition: One element of ``schema`` — a dict with ``segment`` (id string, e.g. ``a_h``), ``kind``, ``n_nodes``, ``lo``/``hi``/``init``/``jit``, and param_mode index lists. Optional axis bags by kind (e.g. ``radius_keys`` for ``a_sti_radius``).
Example: `for segment in schema: ...`, `segment["segment"]`, `slots_for_segment(segment, backend)`
Forbidden: ``seg`` (abbrev); trace ``slice``; CSR ``span``; plot span; bare ``segment`` for non-schema items. Do not store the segment id under ``"name"`` / ``segment_name`` / ``param_name`` — field and locals are ``segment``. Do not use ``node_names`` / ``*_names`` for axis labels — use ``cells`` / ``radius_keys`` / ``pairs`` / ``slots_for_segment``.

### `field` / `fields`

Definition: One column in a rectangular CSV output table.
Example: CSV rectangular output; ``field`` in sidecar/CSV prose for table columns.
Forbidden: ``column`` (for CSV); ``key`` (for ``--param`` token); ``view`` (sti coverage).

### `key` / `keys`

Definition: Which ``optimizable`` attribute a ``--param`` token sets: ``lo`` | ``hi`` | ``jit`` | ``val`` | ``mode``.
Example: `a_h.val.L1=0.5`, `parse_optimizable_tokens`, CLI ``SEGMENT.KEY[.NODES]=VALUE``
Forbidden: ``field`` (for ``--param``); CSV ``field``; dict key in generic prose. Do not call the segment token ``NAME`` / ``name`` in project identifiers.

### `slice` / `slices`

Definition: Contiguous subarray along the trace time axis.
Example: `trace_slice = v_post_d[t_lo:t_hi + 1]`
Forbidden: ``segment``; ``seg`` (for trace subarrays).

### `view` / `views`

Definition: Sti coverage extent on the hex lattice, measured in degrees ``(x0, y0, x1, y1)``.
Example: `view_bounds(hexes)`, `view_deg`, pack info ``"view_deg"``
Forbidden: ``field`` (for sti geometry); ``extent``; bare ``field_deg`` / ``field_bounds``.

### `scale` / `scales`

Definition: A non-``a_*`` multiplicative coefficient — how much a quantity counts or is stretched outside network ``a_*`` params. Primary uses: cost contribution (`cost_scales`, `part_cost_scales`, `dsi_scales`, `spot_cost_radius_scale`, `_part_scale`), RF coefficients (`RF_surrnd_scale`), and other non-edge multipliers. Compounds: `<role>_scale` / plural bag `scales` / `part_cost_scales`.
Example: `pack.cost_scales`, `session.part_cost_scales`, `--part-cost-scale`, `SPOT_COST_RADIUS_SCALE`, `_scaled_cost_from_parts`, `backward_accumulate_scaled_cost`
Forbidden: `weight` for this role (`cost_weight`, …); `factor`; `gain`. Do not use `scale` for network params — use `a_*`. Do not use `scale` for edge synaptic strength — use `weight` / `edge_weights`. Do not use `scale` for schema z↔param encoding — use `z_mode`. Do not use `scale` for Euler / membrane \(\Delta t/\tau\) step factors (`hp_scale`, `lp_scale`, `*_alpha`, …) — write the implicit|explicit update with \(\Delta t/\tau\) directly. Library matplotlib `yscale` / `set_yscale` may remain.

### `session` / `sessions`

Definition: A run-time context object that holds assembled configuration/state for one train/evaluation run (schema/backend/tasks/readouts/opts).
Example: `session = train.session_from_outdir(...)`

### `sign` / `signs`

Definition: Multiplicative ±1 (or label ``+`` / ``−`` / ``0``) encoding the direction of a quantity — synapse E/I (`syn_sign`), cell ON/OFF (`RF_SIGN` / `rf_sign`), bright/dark sti (`contrast_sign`), mirror GT (`mirror_sign`), peak deflection (`v_post_d_sign`). Compounds: `<role>_sign`.
Example: `contrast_sign("dark") == -1`, `rf_sign * contrast_sign(contrast) > 0`, `syn_sign`, `mirror_sign=-1.0`
Forbidden: `polarity` (including `polarity_sign` and prose “polarity”). Do not use `sign` for non-±1 mode enums — that is `mode` (`spot_gt_mode`, not `spot_gt_sign`).

### `spot` / `spots`

Definition: The spot sti paradigm (and its related task variants) used to generate inputs and corresponding targets.
Example: `if task_name in SPOT_TASKS: ...`

### `sti` / `stis`

Definition: Light sti drive — photoreceptor current injected at R1–6/R7/R8 (`i_sti`), and the connectome node flag/mask for those nodes (`is_sti`, JSON `"sti"`, `sti_nodes_at`, `sti_node_indices`, `STI_CELLS`). Compounds cover sti hex anchors and opts (`sti_uv`, `sti_opts`, `entry_sti_us`, `cost_sti_us`, `sti_batch_idx`).
Example: `i_sti = pack.i_sti`, `connectome.is_sti`, `connectome.sti_node_indices`, `STI_CELLS`, `spot_sti_batches`, `build_spot_sti_opts`
Forbidden: `stimulus`, `stim`, `stimuli`, `input`, `is_input`, `PR`, `photoreceptor` (as naming tokens for this concept), `u_in`, `v_in`, `pr_idx`, `n_input_nodes`, `INPUT_CELLS`, `input_nodes_at`. Do not use `sti` for total membrane input (sti + synaptic) — use `in`.

### `sum` / `sums`

Definition: A running total of values (or a bag of such totals keyed by component/cell), produced by accumulate — not the accumulate action itself.
Example: `forward_sums.sums`, `sums[cell] += ...`, `n_syn_sum`, `part_sums`
Forbidden: do not name the bag with a verb (`*_from_accumulate`, `accum`, `acc`). The bag is `sums`; the action is `accumulate`. Do not use `total` for a sum bag — `total` is the scaled cost scalar only. Do not use `count` / `counts` for a sum or cardinality.

### `sum_sq` / `sum_sqs`

Definition: A running total of squared values (or a bag of such totals), kept alongside `sum` / `sums` so std can be recovered.
Example: `sum_sqs[component_key]`, `_std_from_sum_and_sum_sq`
Forbidden: `sumsq`, `accsq`, `accum_sq` — use `sum_sq` / `sum_sqs`.

### `syn` / `syns`

Definition: A synapse in the connectome: the biological connection between two cells, characterized by its sign (`syn_sign`) and count (`n_syn`), from which edge weights are derived.
Example: `syn_sign = float(e["syn_sign"])` — from `network.json` connectome data
Forbidden: do not use `edge` to refer to a synapse or its biological properties. `syn` is a connectome biological entity; `edge` is its network instantiation.

### `t` / `n_t`

Definition: Time sample index along the simulation trace axis — a position 0..`n_t`-1 into `(B, n_t, N)` tensors such as `trace_full`, or an absolute step such as `t_onset` / `cost_t0s`. The cardinality of that axis is `n_t` (how many time samples at one scope). Use compound `n_t_<noun>` when the scope is not obvious (`n_t_aligned`, `n_t_by_batch`, `n_t_cost_window`, `n_t_plot`).
Example: `for t in range(n_t): ...`, `trace_full[:, t, node_idx]`, `t0 + t`, `n_t = int(pack.gts.shape[1])`, `t_from_ms(ms_pre, delta_ms=delta_ms_pre)`
Forbidden: `win`, `win_len`, `win_lens`, `wl`, `win_idx`, `win_t`, `n_steps` — never abbreviate a time span or trace length with `win`; use `n_t` or `n_t_<noun>`. Do not suffix the time index with `_idx` — use bare `t` in loops and indexing (`for t in ...`, `trace[..., t, ...]`), not `t_idx`, `step_idx`, or `time_idx`. Full-word `window` remains only in established compound names (`cost_window_*`, `TimeWindow`, `time_window`, `window_time_traces`).

### `task` / `tasks`

Definition: A train/evaluation task type (e.g., `spot_bright`, `spot_dark`, `moving_bar_bright`, `moving_bar_dark`).
Example: `for name in session.tasks: ...`

### `total`

Definition: The single scaled overall train cost scalar — ``Σ W·part / Σ W`` over parts with non-zero scale (``W`` from `part_cost_scales` / `_part_scale`) — and plot titles/curves that refer to that scalar only.
Example: `total = _scaled_cost_from_parts(parts, session)`, `_last_total`, `"total (scaled)"` on the cost curve
Forbidden: do not use for cardinalities (`n_*`), accumulated value bags (`sums`), plot scope/all traces, durations, or sidecar field labels.

### `trace` / `traces`

Definition: A time sequence of a signal (e.g., voltage readout waveform) represented as a tensor over time indices.
Example: `trace_full, onset_trace = _forward_readout_and_onset_trace(...)`

### `train`

Definition: Noun only — the overall parameter-optimization process and its package (`import train`: session assembly, cost, optimization, persistence). Compounds use the noun (`train_opts`, `param_modes`, `TrainSession`).
Example: `session = train.session_from_outdir(...)`, `import train`
Forbidden: do not use `train` as a verb (e.g. `train_staged`, “to train”, imperative “Train …”) — the update loop verb is `optimize`. Do not use `training` as a naming token — use `train`. Do not use `fit` for this process / package (see `fit` for the GT/cost cell sense only).

### `val` / `vals`

Definition: A short-lived scalar value in a loop or inline expression (not a named param).
Example: `for key, val in opts.items(): out[key] = float(val)`
Forbidden: ``vf``, ``VF``, ``vf_opts``, or any other abbreviation of opts/CLI ``val_from`` surfaces.

### `val_from`

Definition: Train-opts map (CLI ``--val-from``) from target schema segment name to ``{source, enabled}`` — when enabled, the target parameter value is taken from another segment at assignment time and the target is frozen. Bag ``VAL_FROM``; opts key ``val_from``.
Example: `resolve_val_from(args.val_from)`, `parse_val_from_tokens(...)`, `override_val_from(...)`, `val_from_enabled(opts, "bias_gt")`, `resolve_param_modes(param_modes, opts)`, `{"val_from": val_from}`
Forbidden: ``vf``, ``VF``, ``vf_opts``, or any other abbreviation. **Verb exception:** only ``val_from`` compounds may use a lexicon verb prefix (``resolve_val_from``, ``parse_val_from_tokens``, ``override_val_from``, ``add_val_from_argument``, …); this exception does not extend to other ``_from_`` identifiers.

### `weight` / `weights`

Definition: Signed synaptic strength on a network `edge` — `edge_weights` from `syn_sign * n_syn` (`per_cell`) or `syn_sign` (`per_edge`), then split into excitatory/inhibitory drives (further multiplied by `a_syn_exc` / `a_syn_inh`).
Example: `edge_weights`, `ScatterConn(..., edge_weights=edge_weights, a_syn_exc=..., a_syn_inh=...)`
Forbidden: do not use `weight` for cost / DSI / RF multipliers — use `scale`. Do not use `weight` for network schema gains — use `a_*`. Do not use `factor` or `gain` as substitutes. Column-assignment vote mode is `vote_by_syn` (not `weight_by_syn`). Library `fontweight` may remain.

## Adjectives (A-Z)

### `active`

Definition: Modifier marking membership in the subset picked from a candidate pool — the item participates in the current scope. Realized as a list/set/tuple, predicate, or index list, not as a dense per-element 0/1 boolean array. Compound as `<noun>_active`, `active_<noun>`, or `_active_<noun>_…` in function names.
Example: `active_gt_cells(...)`, `spot_gt_active(...)`, `_pack_active_batch_indices(...)`, `active_stis_for_subtype(...)`, `all_active`
Forbidden: `present` for this role. Do not use `active` where the value is a dense boolean 0/1 array along an axis — use `mask` in `<axis>_mask` compounds instead. Do not combine `active` and `mask` in one identifier (`*_active_*_mask`).

## Verbs (A-Z)

### `accumulate`

Definition: Fold many contributions into running sums (sums / sum_sqs / n_nodes bags) or into a target tensor / cost / gradient — the action of adding into an accumulator bag or destination.
Example: `_accumulate_on_target`, `_accumulate_moving_bar_traces`, `backward_accumulate_scaled_cost`, `accumulate_all`
Forbidden: `aggregate` — do not use as a naming token or prose substitute for this action. Do not abbreviate the verb as `acc` / `accum` in names. Noun bags holding accumulated values are `sums` / `sum_sqs` / `n_*`, not verb-derived names.

### `add`

Definition: Register a new argument or option onto a CLI parser object.
Example: `add_train_arguments(parser)`, `add_plot_filter_argument(parser)`
Forbidden: do not use `add` for tensor arithmetic or dict insertion — `add` is exclusively for parser argument registration.

### `override`

Definition: Verb only — merge new values into an existing object (mutate in place or return the updated copy). The payload being merged is named by what it is (``inits``, ``modes``, ``param_inits``, ``pack_mirror_fits``, sti opts, …), never by this verb.
Example: `override_pack(pack, pack_mirror_fit, backend)`, `override_param_modes(...)`, `override_params(...)`, `override_val_from(...)`, `maybe_override_sti_timing(...)`
Forbidden: `apply` (including ``apply_*``) for this merge action; using ``override`` as a noun (``init_override``, ``pack_overrides``, ``param_overrides``, bare ``overrides``, ``*_override`` bags); `edit` / `edits` / ``*_edits`` for the same merge/payload role.

### `build`

Definition: Create/construct a new object/structure in memory from inputs (not necessarily persistent).
Example: `spot = build_spot(connectome, spot_radius=..., multi_spot=..., fully_inside=...)`
Forbidden: `make` — use `build` instead.

### `expand`

Definition: Expand shorthand/alias inputs into a concrete list/dictionary of explicit targets.
Example: `tl = expand_tasks(list(tasks))`

### `inject`

Definition: Arithmetically add a parameter-scaled contribution into an existing tensor in-place (returning the modified tensor).
Example: `i_sti = inject_a_sti_radius(i_sti, p, pack)`
Forbidden: `apply` / `override` — use `inject` only for arithmetic tensor injection; use `override` for merge into an existing object.

### `forward`

Definition: Run the full time-loop simulation on `i_sti` to produce a `(B, T, N)` readout tensor (`v` or `ca`).
Example: `v = forward_v(session, p, i_sti, pack=pack)`
Forbidden: `simulate`, `compute` — use `forward` instead.

### `load`

Definition: Load a saved artifact or serialized object from storage and reconstruct it for use.
Example: `z = train.load_best_param(outdir, session)`
Forbidden: `read` — use `load` instead.

### `normalize`

Definition: Convert an input value into a canonical/valid form (e.g., clamp rules, ordering, or standard representation).
Example: `mode = normalize_syn_mode(syn_mode)`, `normalize_i_sti(session, params, i_sti, pack)` → `(B, T, N)`
Forbidden: `canonicalize`, `standardize`, `prepare` — use `normalize` instead. Do not name this hook `prep` (see `prep` for plot timing only). Do not use `normalize_<noun>` to merge `default_params` bags with CLI tokens — that assembly is `resolve` (`resolve_param_modes`, `resolve_train_sti_timing`).

### `optimize`

Definition: Run an Adam / staged-lr procedure that updates ``z`` to minimize `cost`.
Example: `z_best, opt_state = optimize_staged(z, cost_fn, bounds, lrs, n_iter, ...)`
Forbidden: `fit`, `train` (as a verb) — use `optimize` for the update loop. Do not name the returned ``z`` or post-optimize cost parts with `fit` (`z_fit`, `fit_parts`) — use `z_best`, `final_parts`.

### `parse`

Definition: Decode a raw string or token sequence into a structured in-memory value.
Example: `parse_moving_bar_spec(sname)` → `(color, direction, variant)`

### `plot`

Definition: Produce a visualization output (figures/curves/images) from run results or computed traces.
Example: `plot_cost(result.cost_curve, out_path, costs_by_part=...)`
Forbidden: `draw`, `render`, `visualize`, and project names built on `scatter` (e.g. “Δv scatters”) — use `plot` instead. Library `ax.scatter` / `go.Scatter` may remain.

### `resolve`

Definition: Resolve abstract references/aliases/defaults into final concrete values used by execution. Use when the answer is which value or config to run with — inputs may be incomplete (CLI tokens, alias names, branch dicts, omitted keys) and the function consults ``default_params``, alias tables, and/or CLI tokens before returning.
Example: `out[tname] = int(expanded[tname])`, `resolve_val_from(args.val_from)`, `resolve_param_modes(param_modes, opts)`, `resolve_run_kwargs(args)`, `resolve_train_sti_timing(filter, args.sti_timing)`, `resolve_train_kwargs(args)`, `resolve_sti_timing(opts)`, `resolve_tasks(tasks)`
Forbidden: `lookup`, `fetch` — use `resolve` instead. Do not name default+CLI-token assembly with `copy_<noun>` or project `copy_*` helpers — use `resolve_<noun>`. Library `copy.deepcopy` at persistence boundaries may remain. Do not name resolve work ``parse_*``, ``build_*``, ``merge_*``, ``default_*``, or ``*_from_*`` (``train_kwargs_from_args``, ``sti_timing_from_opts``, ``parse_part_cost_scale``, ``build_train_opts``) — use ``resolve_<noun>``. ``parse_*`` may decode raw strings to structured tokens only; ``expand_*`` may expand aliases only — when defaults and CLI tokens are merged into the final execution value, that step is ``resolve`` (see ``### normalize``). Do not use ``normalize_*`` for this assembly.

### `run`

Definition: Execute a procedure end-to-end (train, evaluation, or full pipeline) and return or persist its result.
Example: `fname, outdir, session = run_train_and_plot(plot_gts=..., **kw)`
Forbidden: `execute`, `invoke` — use `run` instead.

### `save`

Definition: Persist in-memory arrays/objects to disk in a machine-recoverable form (e.g., `.npy/.npz` or serialized state).
Example: `np.savez_compressed(path, hex_current=hex_current)`
Forbidden: `write`, `dump`, `export` — use `save` instead.

### `step`

Definition: Advance the neuron state by one time sample (`t-1 → t`) using the model dynamics.
Example: `state, v = drv.step(state, v, p, i_sti[:, t-1], session, delta_ms=...)`
Forbidden: do not use `step` to mean an optimizer iteration — use `iter` instead.

## Prepositions (A-Z)

### `_from_`

Definition: The sole directional preposition in function names and map attribute/local names — a deterministic map from one already-concrete source to one result (unit/coordinate conversion, schema pack/unpack, idx lookup, fixed formula on present inputs). Pattern: `<result>_from_<source>`; both sides nouns only; the full identifier has no verb token. This entry's Definition and Example lines use nouns only — no verb tokens.
Example: `t_from_ms(ms, delta_ms)`, `v_ca_from_v(v, p, session)`, `ca_from_v_ca(...)`, `t_abs_from_ms(...)`, `node_values_from_z(z, schema)`, `hex_from_uv(u, v)`, `xy_deg_from_uv(u, v)`, `cell_from_node(...)`, `node_from_id`, `family_from_cell_csv(...)`
Forbidden: `_to_` — never use `_to_` as a directional preposition in function names or map attribute/local names. Any verb token anywhere in the identifier (`load`, `override`, `open`, `resolve`, `parse`, `build`, `assert`, …). Verbs in this lexicon entry's Definition or Example lines. Exception: ``val_from`` compounds only (see ``### val_from``). Do not use ``_from_`` when the function merges ``default_params``, alias expansion, and/or CLI tokens into the final execution value — that is ``resolve`` (``resolve_train_kwargs``, ``resolve_sti_timing``, ``resolve_spot``), not ``kwargs_from_args``, ``timing_from_opts``, or ``spot_from_opts``. Do not use ``_from_`` for multi-source assembly (defaults + tokens + validation); one complete source → one result only. Simple field extraction from an already-final opts dict (``gt_cells_from_opts``) may remain ``_from_``; if missing keys fall back to defaults, rename to ``resolve_*``.
